  "reason": "The set provides a plausible route to the later result. R1 establishes mechanical alloying followed by SPS at 900–1000 °C, including 1000 °C, for a closely related Ti-containing transition-metal HEA, while R3 uses SPS at 1050 °C on the especially similar AlCoCrFeNiTi0.5 system and reports a dense microstructure plus spinel formation. R2, R5, R6, R7, and R9 show that mechanically alloyed BCC HEA powders consolidated by SPS commonly develop oxide or other secondary phases whose population and properties depend on processing temperature. Independently, R12 and R13 directly connect protective oxide formation in compositionally related HEAs with parabolic, diffusion-controlled oxidation behavior, and several records address chloride corrosion or passive/protective scales. An expert could therefore reasonably propose SPS near 1000 °C for ball-milled MgAlTiVFeCo and expect densification together with oxide barriers to improve hardness, oxidation, and corrosion resistance, although the exact optimum, named AlV2O4/FeAl2O4/FeTiO3 phases, and detailed chloride-defense behavior would still require experiment."
